# nomo snapshot v1
# worksheet: prose

=== text ===
# Prose in a worksheet
A comment is Markdown. This worksheet is what pins that: every construct
below is in the golden snapshot beside it, so a change to how prose renders
shows up here in the diff.
## Paragraphs
Consecutive comment lines are one paragraph. Prose wraps at whatever width
the file is written to, and comes out as a paragraph rather than as a stack
of one-line ones.

A blank line starts the next paragraph, and so does a bare comment marker
like the one above this line.
## Headings
`#` through `######`, with a space after the marker. A level-1 heading at the
top of a worksheet names the document: `nomo html` takes the page's title
from there rather than from the file name.
## Lists
Bullets take `-`, `*` or `+`:
- the marker needs a space after it
- an item continues onto the lines under it, so a long one wraps
  like this rather than becoming a second item
- and there is no nesting: indentation is a wrap
Numbered items take `1.` or `1)` and keep the number they were written with.
That matters because a worksheet numbers its steps with the mathematics
between them, so each step arrives on its own:
1. State the load and the supply.
P_load = 3.6 kW
V_line = 230 V
I_load = P_load/V_line = 3.6 kW/(230 V) = 15.6522 A
2. Size the conductor from the current. Six amps per square millimetre is
   what this installation works to.
J_max = 6 A/mm²
A_min = I_load/J_max = 15.6522 A/(6000000 m⁻²·A) = 2.6087e-6 m²
A_min = 2.6087 mm²
3. Check the drop over the run at the section actually installed.
rho = 0.0175 ohm·mm²/m
L_run = 35 m
A_used = 4 mm²
V_drop = 2·rho·L_run·I_load/A_used = 2·(1.75e-8 m³·kg·s⁻³·A⁻²)·(35 m)·(15.6522 A)/(4e-6 m²) = 4.79348 V
drop_ratio = V_drop/V_line = 4.79348 V/(230 V) = 0.0208412
## What stays prose
The subset is closed. Three things are outside it on purpose, and this is
what they look like when a worksheet writes them:
The three dashes on the next line are text, not a rule.
---
There are no thematic breaks and no setext headings, because
`' --- resources ---` is what opens the resource trailer and a worksheet must
not acquire a section break by writing about a range of values.
Leading whitespace is a wrap and not a code block, so a line
    indented like this one joins the line above it.
#3 is a screw size rather than a heading, because a heading needs a space
after its marker.
A backslash protects a marker that was meant literally, so the line below is
prose rather than a one-item list:

\- 40 °C is the lower limit of the qualified range.
## And none of it is markup
Prose is escaped on its way into the document. A worksheet may have been
written by the SMath importer out of a file nobody here wrote, so <b>this</b>
is four characters and a word, not bold text.

