<h1 class="prose">Prose in a worksheet</h1>
<p class="prose">A comment is Markdown. This worksheet is what pins that: every construct below is in the golden snapshot beside it, so a change to how prose renders shows up here in the diff.</p>
<h2 class="prose">Paragraphs</h2>
<p class="prose">Consecutive comment lines are one paragraph. Prose wraps at whatever width the file is written to, and comes out as a paragraph rather than as a stack of one-line ones.</p>
<p class="prose">A blank line starts the next paragraph, and so does a bare comment marker like the one above this line.</p>
<h2 class="prose">Headings</h2>
<p class="prose">`#` through `######`, with a space after the marker. A level-1 heading at the top of a worksheet names the document: `nomo html` takes the page's title from there rather than from the file name.</p>
<h2 class="prose">Lists</h2>
<p class="prose">Bullets take `-`, `*` or `+`:</p>
<ul class="prose">
<li>the marker needs a space after it</li>
<li>an item continues onto the lines under it, so a long one wraps like this rather than becoming a second item</li>
<li>and there is no nesting: indentation is a wrap</li>
</ul>
<p class="prose">Numbered items take `1.` or `1)` and keep the number they were written with. That matters because a worksheet numbers its steps with the mathematics between them, so each step arrives on its own:</p>
<ol class="prose">
<li>State the load and the supply.</li>
</ol>
